WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.253
Variance measures spread around a mean using squared deviations. Covariance measures whether two centred quantities tend

00:00:07.253 --> 00:00:14.307
to have deviations of the same sign. Its value depends on their units; correlation divides out

00:00:14.307 --> 00:00:16.070
their marginal standard deviations.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:23.090
Variance measures squared spread around each variable’s mean. Here both variables have unit standard deviation.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:30.740
The off-diagonal entry is covariance. With unit marginal variances it also equals the correlation coefficient.

00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:36.707
Can the variance of a sum be smaller than the variance of either individual measurement? Pause

00:00:36.707 --> 00:00:39.087
here and make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:48.285
The covariance term matters because the variables can move together or offset each other. Adding variances

00:00:48.285 --> 00:00:56.369
alone assumes zero covariance. For our starting inputs, Correlation: 0.5. Variance of sum: 3. SD of

00:00:56.369 --> 00:00:57.380
sum: 1.7321.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.265
Standard deviation is the square root of variance. It has the same units as the summed

00:01:03.265 --> 00:01:03.600
measurement.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:10.669
Measurement noise can be correlated across pixels and channels. Do not automatically treat every variance as

00:01:10.669 --> 00:01:11.080
additive.

